Ladybird paints a translucent root background color once over the
canvas: in the root element’s own background, or, for a hidden root, in
the canvas fill. Inside a transformed or sticky root, it still paints it
twice, as before: The root keeps its own fill, where its filter and
blend modes apply, and the canvas fill covers the rest of the canvas.
Blink paints it once, from the view, in its ViewPainter::
PaintRootElementGroup(). WebKit paints it from the view, in
RenderView::paintBoxDecorations(), and its RenderBox treats the document
element’s background as special, rather than painting it again. Gecko
paints it once, from the canvas frame, with the style it gets from
nsCSSRendering::FindRootFrameBackground().

Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.78, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5, a root with
a 40% blue background comes out rgb(153, 153, 255) in all three — with a
blur filter, visibility: hidden, a propagated body background, an image
layer, or background-clip, as it now does in Ladybird. With transform:
scale(0.5), the engines show it once everywhere; Ladybird still shows it
twice inside the scaled root. They differ with the root’s opacity:
Chrome applies it to the canvas background (rgb(204, 204, 255)), and
Firefox and WebKit don’t (rgb(153, 153, 255)). And with filter:
invert(1), Chrome inverts the color alone, while Firefox and WebKit
invert the composited canvas. Ladybird now matches Chrome in both.
